{-
   The run on JS - and why its slice is Implementation-Containers.md §4.3's three components.

   `items` is the whole host array in every slice of it, so a window needs a base of its own and
   that base is `offset`, the third field of `Core/Array.js.yana`'s `ArrayView`. `Run.yana` says why
   it is last rather than second; `Core/Array.native.yana`'s is the two-field form, where the
   pointer already is the window.

   No `values` on this target, and that is the stronger half of the same fact: handing a caller
   `items` would hand them the whole array, which is a read past both ends of what they asked for.
-}

{-
   A run of `capacity` slots, placed by the compiler - the JS half of `newRun`.

   No body, and there is none to write: a run on this target is a descriptor over a host array the
   compiler places, and `allocateHeap` is `Heap.native.yana`'s. So this side is the role and nothing
   else, and `Run.native.yana` is the side that also carries the fallback body.
-}
@builtin(newRun)
pub fn newRun(capacity: Size) -> Run(a)

-- The record is `Core/Array.js.yana`'s `ArrayView`, beside the owner it is a view of.
